    "action": "Four commits, in this order.\n\n**(a) Title dedupe — 01-REVIEW IN-01.** `src/planning-repo/handlers/generic.ts` declares a local\n`deriveTitle(content, path)` whose body duplicates `handlers/title.ts`'s exported\n`deriveTitleFromHeading`, and its inline frontmatter-title branch duplicates `title.ts`'s exported\n`deriveTitle(frontmatterData, body, path)`. At planning time the two were observed equivalent —\nsame regex, same trim, same path fallback, same `typeof === 'string' && length > 0` guard. Re-read\nboth before collapsing. If they have since diverged in any respect, **report the divergence and do\nnot force them together** — a silent behavior change hiding inside a \"dedupe\" commit is worse than\nthe duplication. If equivalent, delete the local copy and call the shared `deriveTitle` directly.\nByte-identical output is the whole point: `test/__golden__` must pass with nothing regenerated.\n\n**(b) `engines` — 01-REVIEW IN-02.** Derive the floor; do not assume it. Read the `engines` field of\neach installed toolchain package that actually gates this project (`vite`, `vitest`, `eslint`,\n`typescript`, `@vitejs/plugin-react`, `hono`, `@hono/node-server`, `react`, `tailwindcss`) out of\n`node_modules/<pkg>/package.json`, and take the highest floor. Then account for a floor the\ndependency graph does not declare: `npm run snapshot`, `npm run dev` and `npm run start` invoke\n`node src/**/*.ts` directly, relying on Node's native TypeScript type-stripping being on without a\nflag — that has its own minimum Node version, and if it is higher than every package floor then it\nis the effective floor. The project floor cannot be below the highest of these. State each measured\nfloor and the resulting maximum in the commit message. Add no npm constraint unless something you\nmeasured actually requires one. Edit `package.json` only; do not run an install.\n\n**(c) `depends_on` warning.** The warning channel is `WarningCollector` (`planning-repo/warnings.ts`),\nand today it does not reach the cross-reference pass: `assembleDomainModel` takes a\n`_warnings: ParseWarning[]` it ignores, and `resolveCrossReferences(project)` takes no sink at all.\nThread the collector itself (not a flat array snapshot) from `snapshot.ts` through\n`assembleDomainModel` into `resolveCrossReferences`, so a warning added during cross-referencing is\nvisible to `snapshot.ts`'s post-assembly read of the live collector. Decide and record whether the\nnew warning also belongs on the owning artifact's own `warnings` array or only in the flat snapshot\nlist — the per-artifact arrays may already have been materialized before assembly, in which case\nflat-only is the honest answer and you say so rather than pretending otherwise.\n\nTwo invariants are non-negotiable and each needs its own test. **D-12:** `load()` must still not\nthrow on this input — the original fix existed precisely because this value crashed it, so a\nregression here re-opens a closed critical. **D-10:** a well-formed but unresolvable reference must\nstill produce no warning; `test/__golden__/sparse-started.json` records 0 warnings while its fixture\ncarries the dangling `SLICE-99`, so that golden is itself a D-10 silence pin. Add an explicit test\npinning that silence anyway, so the invariant is stated and not merely implied by a fixture.\n\nNo fixture carries a non-array `depends_on`, so **no golden should change**. Verify that with\n`git diff --exit-code -- test/__golden__`. If a golden does legitimately gain this warning, your\npremise was wrong — update that one golden and name it explicitly in the commit and the summary,\nwith the fixture and the reason.\n\nThe commit message must state why this reverses the trade-off documented in `01-REVIEW-FIX.md` and\nin `crossref.ts`'s own header comment: D-10's rule is about *well-formed references to things that\ndo not exist*, which are routine in GSD prose; a *wrong-shaped field* is a different category — it\nis structurally invalid regardless of what it points at, and is therefore distinguishable. Update\n`crossref.ts`'s header comment so the file no longer documents a trade-off it no longer makes; leave\nthe dangling-reference rationale intact, because that part still stands.\n\n**(d) `in-memory-fs` seam.** This is a seam cleanup restoring plan 01-01's must-have that\n`local-fs.ts` is the only file under `src/` importing the Node filesystem module. DATA-01's\nsubstitution guarantee already holds — the interface methods never touch real disk — so nothing\nabout correctness changes. Enumerate every caller of `static fromDirectory()` first (exactly one at\nplanning time: `test/fs-equivalence.test.ts`); if there are now more, all of them move together.\nMove the loader to a test helper. `test/helpers/from-directory.ts` is covered by\n`tsconfig.server.json`'s `test/**/*.ts` include and is not matched by `vitest.config.ts`'s\n`test/**/*.test.ts` collection glob, so it typechecks and imports without being collected as an\nempty suite — confirm both of those still hold rather than taking it on faith. `test/fs-equivalence.test.ts`\nmust pass unchanged in what it asserts; only its import line moves.\n\nThen add the enforcement gate. It must catch the Node filesystem module under every spelling that\nwould actually work — the `node:`-prefixed and bare forms, the `/promises` variant, static `import`,\n`require`, and dynamic `import(...)` — scoped to `src/` only, since `test/portability.test.ts`\nlegitimately imports the promises API. It must not be fooled by prose: `src/cli/target-path.ts`,\n`src/planning-repo/discovery.ts` and `src/server/search-index.ts` all mention the module name in\ncomments, and `local-fs.ts`'s own comment quotes the import string verbatim. Match on import syntax\n(the module specifier in quotes as the argument of an import/require form), not on the bare token,\nand exclude comment lines.\n\nA gate matching only today's literal line is theatre. Prove it is not: temporarily plant a real\nimport in some other file under `src/`, confirm the gate fails, remove the plant, confirm it passes.\nRecord that positive control in the summary. Add the gate as a durable test in the suite — not only\nas a shell one-liner — so it stays enforced after this task; `test/portability.test.ts` already reads\nsource files for structural proofs and is the natural home.",
