    "action": "Three commits, in this order.\n\n**(a) Dead critical-CSS scaffold — 02-REVIEW IN-02.** The audit's \"roughly 150 of 297 lines\" is a\nprior estimate, not authority. Derive the deletion set yourself.\n\nGrep every selector in the `<style>` block against `src/web` with **word-boundary accuracy on the\nclass token**, not substring matching. The planning-time grep was substring-fuzzy and its \"live\"\ncolumn is not trustworthy: `.status` almost certainly matched `snapshot-status`, and `.metadata`\nalmost certainly matched `artifact-metadata`. Both may in fact be dead. Conversely `.eyebrow` and\n`.lede` returned real class-attribute hits and are probably live. Match against actual `className`\ntoken lists — split on whitespace and compare whole tokens.\n\nThen, separately and second, judge each block on **what it does**, because some blocks are\nload-bearing by effect rather than by class name and no grep will catch them:\n\n- The inline `<script>` that reads `localStorage` and toggles the `dark` class runs before first\n  paint. It has no selector at all. It stays.\n- The `:root` and `:root.dark` custom-property blocks paint the pre-hydration ground: they set\n  `color`, `background`, `color-scheme` and `font-family` on the root element itself. Deleting them\n  gives a white flash before the stylesheet loads in dark theme — a visible regression, and exactly\n  the failure no selector grep would catch. They stay. Trim individual tokens inside them only if\n  you can show the token is referenced by nothing that runs before hydration.\n- `* { box-sizing }`, the `body` rule (which carries `min-width: 320px` and the ground paint), and\n  the `button, input, textarea, select { font: inherit }` reset are all pre-hydration effect. They\n  stay unless you can show otherwise.\n\nWhat is left after those two passes is the component scaffold: card, progress, metadata, status,\nerror, skeleton chrome, its keyframes, and the narrow-width media query that only styles those\nselectors. Delete only what is dead by both tests. If a rule is dead by selector but you are unsure\nwhether it is load-bearing by effect, keep it and say why.\n\nPin the anti-FOUC contract with a test so a future cleanup cannot repeat this: assert `index.html`\nstill contains the theme-priming script and still declares a background, a foreground and a\ncolour-scheme under both the light and dark root selectors. Report the actual before/after line\ncount against the measured 297, and whether it matched the audit's estimate.\n\n**(b) The sub-10px micro-label.** Diagnose the chain first. At planning time there is no\n`font-size` declaration on `html`, `:root` or `body` anywhere in `globals.css`, so the root is the\nbrowser default 16px and `0.62rem` is 9.92px **directly** — there is no em compounding to unwind.\nConfirm that on the live tree, because if a root font-size has since appeared the whole diagnosis\nchanges.\n\nThe real root cause is a design convention, not one leaf: an uppercase micro-label size applied as a\nliteral across several selectors. `0.62rem` appears at three (`.artifact-metadata > summary span`,\n`.warning-fields dt`, `.warning-fields-label`) and `0.6rem` — 9.6px, smaller still — at three more.\nEnumerate the live set yourself and **say in the summary which siblings share the root cause**, then\nfix the family rather than bolting an absolute pixel value onto the one leaf the audit happened to\nname. A single named token used by the whole family is the fix that holds; `0.625rem` is exactly\n10px with no headroom against rounding, so prefer a value with margin.\n\nDo not regress contrast. The colour is `var(--muted-foreground)` and size does not change a contrast\nratio, so 4.74 light / 6.99 dark should hold by construction — but measure it rather than reasoning\nto it, since you may end up changing more than size. Verify at narrow widths too, not just desktop:\ngrep for these selectors inside media queries, because a narrow-width override could reintroduce a\nsub-floor size that a desktop-only check would never see.\n\nPin it in the visual-contract suite. The pin must cover the whole cascade including media queries,\nnot just the one base declaration — an assertion that passes while a media query re-shrinks the text\nis worse than no assertion.\n\n**(c) Mermaid palette.** Only five variables are set today, so most of mermaid's palette falls back\nto its own defaults against the app's oklch ground — that is the root of the \"very ugly\" verdict.\nThe 02-13 diagnosis measured the offending diagram background as the dark `--secondary` value, which\ncarries chroma and sits off the app's zero-chroma neutral palette; `--card` is the neutral surface\nthe rest of the app paints on. That is a concrete lead for the node-fill seed.\n\nBefore mapping anything, work out which variables are **seeds** and which mermaid **derives**. Under\n`theme: 'base'` mermaid computes a large part of its palette from a small set of seeds; a mapping\nthat sets a derived value to something inconsistent with its seed can look worse than one that seeds\nwell and lets derivation do its job. Establish the seed set first, seed those deliberately, and set\nderived values explicitly only where derivation demonstrably produces something wrong against this\npalette.\n\nCover: node fill and border, cluster fill and border, the secondary and tertiary colours, edge\ncolour and edge-label background, cluster and node text colour, note background/text/border, and\nfont size. Source every one from an existing CSS custom property read off the document root and\nconvert it through `toMermaidColor()`, which stays the **single** conversion seam — do not add a\nsecond converter, and do not hand mermaid a raw property value unconverted.\n\nTwo traps to check rather than discover at runtime. First, custom properties whose value is a\n`color-mix(...)` expression come back from `getPropertyValue` as that expression, not as a resolved\ncolour; `toMermaidColor()` returns anything it cannot parse unchanged, so such a value reaches\nmermaid's colour library verbatim. Prefer properties whose values are plain `oklch(...)`. Second,\nsome border tokens carry a low alpha in dark theme; converted faithfully that produces a nearly\ninvisible node border. Judge whether the faithful conversion actually reads well and pick something\nthat does if it does not — you own the aesthetic call here and there is no screenshot gate.\n\nKeep intact: all three rungs of the fallback ladder (custom variables, then colourless base options,\nthen per-node readable source), `securityLevel: 'strict'`, and the dynamic `import('mermaid')`.\nPin all three plus the single-seam rule with a source-level contract test: every value in the theme\nvariables object flows through the converter, the strict security level is present, the import is\ndynamic, and both lower fallback rungs are still reachable.",
