    "action": "Measure first, decide second.\n\nRe-run the build and record every chunk over 500 kB with its raw and gzip size. At planning time\nthere were exactly two: the entry chunk at 571.58 kB (gzip 180.76) and a 662.12 kB chunk (gzip\n143.15). The larger one is **not** the entry — it is mermaid's shared internals, imported by\n`mermaid.core`, `mermaid-parser.core` and every one of mermaid's ~18 per-diagram chunks. Mermaid is\nalready behind a dynamic import, so it is already async-only; that is why the audit's instinct to\nsuspect mermaid needs restating precisely rather than dismissing. The advisory fires on chunk size\nregardless of whether the chunk is async, which is the actual reason it appears.\n\nThen check the more interesting question the task scope raises: is any of the markdown pipeline\nreaching the browser bundle at all? It is meant to be server-side only. Grepping minified output for\npackage names is weak evidence and returned nothing conclusive at planning time. The decisive check\nis at the import graph, not the bundle: if nothing under `src/web/**` imports the unified/remark/\nrehype/shiki/minisearch/gray-matter modules, directly or transitively through a module that does,\nthey cannot reach the client entry. Run that check and report the result either way — a positive\nfinding here is more consequential than the advisory itself and would change what you do next.\n\nWith numbers in hand, prefer real splitting. For the entry chunk the obvious candidate is\nroute-level lazy loading of the page components. Evaluate it honestly: measure what it actually buys\non a loopback-only, single-user tool served from localhost, where the request cost that code\nsplitting optimizes is close to zero and the cost is extra suspense boundaries and load states in a\nread-only dashboard. For the mermaid chunk, note that rolldown has already split mermaid into many\nchunks behind one dynamic boundary; further splitting inside it is not something this project\ncontrols without fighting the bundler.\n\nIf, after measuring, splitting buys nothing that matters here, raising the chunk-size warning\nthreshold is the acceptable resolution — but only then, and only with a comment in `vite.config.ts`\nthat records the measured numbers, the date, and why the threshold is set where it is. Set it just\nabove the largest measured chunk, not at some round number far above it, so a real future regression\nstill trips it.\n\nThree ways of making the advisory disappear are forbidden because they suppress output rather than\nresolve it: lowering the build's log verbosity, passing a quiet flag to the build script, and\nredirecting or filtering the build's output. Do not use any of them, and do not restate their option\nnames in a code comment in `vite.config.ts` — the verification greps that file for them, and a\ncomment naming them would fail the check for no reason.\n\nReport before and after numbers in the summary.",
