    "action": "Create `frontend/lib/password-rules.ts` as a pure, framework-free module: `MIN_PASSWORD_LENGTH` of 12 and `MAX_PASSWORD_LENGTH` of 128 mirroring the server's own constants, `validateNewPassword(next: string, confirm: string)` returning a discriminated `PasswordRuleFailure` or nothing, and `CHANGE_PASSWORD_COPY` holding the screen's strings verbatim from the UI contract's Copywriting Contract. The rule is length only, with no composition requirement of any kind — that is current OWASP and NIST guidance, and composition rules push people toward predictable substitutions while blocking good passphrases. When both rules fail, return the too-short failure: it is the rule the member must satisfy first, and stacking two errors on a two-field form is noise. Write the matching node test file in the style `frontend/lib/theme.test.ts` uses.\n\nCreate `frontend/app/change-password/page.tsx` as a client component matching `/login`'s structure, spacing, and spinner convention exactly — the same centered card, the same fixed alert region above the fields, the same house spinner bound directly to the pending request with no artificial delay. It renders the card title, a description explaining that the account carries a temporary password, then the alert region, then a new-password field and a confirm-password field, then the primary button.\n\nValidation fires on submit only — never on blur and never per keystroke. A mismatch error shown while the confirm field is partially typed would be wrong for the entire time the member is typing toward a correct value, which is always. The length rule is communicated up front by an always-visible helper line beneath the new-password field, so submit-only validation is not a surprise: the requirement was on screen before the member typed a character. Submit stays disabled until both fields have content, and a form with one field filled shows no error, identically to `/login`.\n\nBoth fields cap at 128 characters, matching the login password cap and the server's own limit, with the same caveat that a client attribute is an affordance and the backend enforces independently.\n\nThe screen's own minimal header carries a log-out control alongside the theme toggle — D-12 exempts logout from the forced-change gate precisely so a member who cannot recall the temporary password an operator gave them is not trapped with no escape hatch.\n\nOn success, navigate straight to `/browse` with no confirmation banner, toast, or interstitial screen: arriving where they were going is the confirmation, and this codebase ships no toast library. On failure, render the backend's error in the same fixed alert region. Arriving at this screen is a silent, expected transition — do not render an alert or toast on entry telling the member they must change their password first; it is normal routing, not a failure state.\n\nTake every visible string from `CHANGE_PASSWORD_COPY`; write no user-facing literal inline in the page.",
