    "action": "Have `frontend/proxy.ts` write a `portal_profile` cookie on every response it allows through, carrying the handle and display name it already received from the identity call it makes on that same request. This cookie is script-readable by design and carries no credential — the session cookie stays HttpOnly and is untouched. Writing it in the gate rather than at login keeps it in step with the validated session, refreshes it whenever a display name changes, and means the header needs no fetch of its own, which is what makes the no-loading-state contract for this slot true rather than aspirational.\n\nRead it in `frontend/components/app-chrome.tsx` through a `readProfileCookie()` helper and pass the resulting values to the nav shell as props. Resolve it in a state initializer so the value is present on the client's very first render, mirroring how this app already resolves the theme before paint, and mark the rendered span to tolerate the server-rendered-empty to client-rendered-populated difference the same way the root element already does for the theme class. Do not add a fetch, an effect-driven load, or a skeleton for this slot — the UI contract dismisses a loading state here on the grounds that no client-side fetch exists, and adding one would make that reasoning false.\n\nIn `frontend/components/nav-shell.tsx`, insert the two new elements into the existing right-hand cluster, positioned before the theme toggle so the connection indicator stays rightmost and most prominent — the file's own locked ordering rationale, and the display name is the least time-sensitive element in the header. Add a vertical separator in the variant already used on the left side of this header, hidden below 640px alongside the rest of the compact-viewport treatment.\n\nThe display name span truncates past its maximum width rather than wrapping or pushing the connection indicator out of position, and the whole span is hidden below 640px, matching the narrow-viewport discipline already in this file. When the member has no display name set — including a bootstrap account created before any profile exists — render their login handle in the same span. There is no third rendering: the slot is never blank and never carries a placeholder string, because the handle is always available and is more informative than either alternative.\n\nThe log-out control is a ghost icon button in the same size the adjacent theme toggle already uses, with a log-out icon from the project's existing icon library, an accessible label, and screen-reader-only text. Match the neighbouring control rather than introducing a second touch-target rule one element away from it. There is no confirmation dialog: logging out is low-consequence and instantly reversible by signing back in, and the project's destructive-confirmation component is reserved for irreversible data loss.\n\nIts click behavior, in order: post to the backend log-out route with credentials included; close the app's own WebSocket, which is the frontend's half of D-04's \"immediately\"; then perform a full-page navigation to `/login`, not a client-router push, so the route gate and the socket provider both re-evaluate from a real request rather than a stale client tree.\n\nIf the post fails for any reason, still close the socket and still navigate. Do not stay put, do not surface an error alert in the header, and do not retry. The route gate re-evaluates on arrival and is the sole authority on the outcome: a genuinely-ended session lands on the login form, while a session the server never actually ended simply routes the member back into the app. This fails toward signed-out on the client and never leaves a member who explicitly asked to leave holding a live session with only an error message to show for it. The one accepted consequence is that a failed post may leave a server-side session alive until it expires on its own — a backend-availability problem no header alert can fix.",
